// Test: decision 12 of Divergente_ES/forma/README.md — the rule of the result.
//
//   · a `$` edit whose result is USED builds, and the original is untouched;
//   · a `$` edit that IS the whole statement modifies in place.
//
// The two cases are disjoint and are told apart by looking at the syntax. And
// discarding the result has no other possible use: if you were going to throw it
// away, you meant to modify.
//
// Before this, a bare `arr$+ 3` ran and did nothing at all, with no warning
// (DI-01), and `arr[2]$~ 99` as a statement did not even parse in the two Rust
// engines — so with `arr[i] = v` on its way out there was NO way to change an
// element in place. That is why this rule comes before the withdrawal.

// ── the result is used: builds, original intact ──────────────────────────────
a = [1, 2, 3]
b = a$+ 4
>> a " · " b ¶

c = a[2]$~ 99
>> a " · " c ¶

// ── the result is discarded: modifies in place ───────────────────────────────
d = [1, 2, 3]
d$+ 4
>> d ¶

d[2]$~ 99
>> d ¶

d$-[1]
>> d ¶

d$++ 7 8
>> d ¶

// The deep form modifies too, through the same navigation.
m = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
m[1>2]$~ 77
>> m ¶

// ── the dictionary is mutable, and this is how it is modified ────────────────
// Decisions 7-11: the named tuple has the dictionary model. The tree-walker used
// to refuse this — it guarded named tuples as immutable — while the VM allowed
// it (DM-21); the tree-walker is the one that came into line.
u = #(nombre: "Ana", edad: 30)
u["edad"]$~ 31
>> u ¶

// ── a positional tuple does not change, whatever the operator ────────────────
// Refusals are pinned in reject/tuples/01, 02 and 03. What belongs here is the
// other half: the functional forms still build, exactly as (1,2) + (3,) does in
// Python.
t = (1, 2)
t2 = t$+ 3
>> t " · " t2 ¶

t3 = t[1]$~ 9
>> t " · " t3 ¶
